  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...First accumulating snow for parts of the Alleghenies with\nrain showers and brisk conditions elsewhere, today through \nSaturday.\n\nShowers and perhaps a thunderstorm are expected today into early\nthis evening, mainly in the 18Z-00Z time frame as low pressure\ntracks across the area. Cdfnt will shift east of the area after\n00Z tonight with brisk NW winds developing and temperatures\nfalling from the low 60s into the mid 50s by early evening and\ninto the mid 40s by daybreak Fri. Across the higher elevs,\ntemperatures will fall much more quickly into the upper 20s or\nlow 30s by Fri morning. Off and on snow showers are anticipated\nacross the mountains late tonight into Sunday morning. Despite\nplenty of cold air to support snow, snow accumulations have\ncontinued to trend downward as a result of unfavorable low-level\ntrajectories. This still looks like a light snow event of 1 to \n2 inches falling over a 24-hr period, which does not meet the \ncriteria for a Winter Weather Advisory. Elsewhere, light\nrainfall accumulations of a tenth to two tenths of an inch are\nexpected, mainly this afternoon. \n\nContinued brisk conditions are expected into Monday with plenty\nof stratocumulus clouds during the daytime hours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler temperatures and dry condtions expected into \nthe middle of next week.\n\nLongwave troughing will remain over the east coast through early \nnext week, although heights will start rising Monday as the \namplified upper level pattern slowly progresses eastward. \nLikewise, low level trajectories will gradually become less \nfavorable for upslope snow showers Sunday. Winds should be much \nless noticeable by Monday and Tuesday as the surface high \nfinally reaches the Mid Atlantic, although temperatures remain \nbelow normal. Given the lighter winds, the subsequent nights \ncould turn out colder in many areas, with widespread near or \nbelow freezing temperatures outside of the urban centers and \ntidal shoreline.\n\nFurther upper level amplification will likely happen toward mid \nweek. While a trough or low could develop toward the southeast \ncoast, it appears that ridging will dominate the Mid Atlantic. This \nshould start a warming trend beginning Wednesday.",
